<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean-François Champollion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1790">1790</a>–<a href="/years/1832">1832</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Champollion le jeune, Champollion</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean-François Champollion was a French philologist and orientalist, known as the decipherer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptian-hieroglyphs">Egyptian hieroglyphs</a> and a founding figure of Egyptology. A child prodigy in philology, as a young man he read Coptic, Ancient Greek, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and Arabic. In 1822 he published his first breakthrough in reading the hieroglyphs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosetta-stone">Rosetta Stone</a>, showing that Egyptian writing combined phonetic and ideographic signs, and in 1824 his Précis set out the values of its signs. In 1829 he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and read many texts never studied before. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1832, aged 41; some faulted him for not crediting the earlier work of Thomas Young.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-francois-champollion.jpg" alt="Jean-François Champollion" width="330" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Léon Cogniet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean-François_Champollion,_by_Léon_Cogniet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its decipherment by <b>Jean-François Champollion</b> in 1822, and discoveries such as the tomb of Tutankhamun, found intact by Howard Carter in 1922, gave birth to Egyptology and to waves of popular fascination with Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-egyptian-religion">Ancient Egyptian religion · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The English scholar Thomas Young made progress with demotic and the royal names, and in 1822 <b>Jean-François Champollion</b>, who knew Coptic well, showed that hieroglyphs combined signs for sounds and for meanings, reading the names of Ptolemy and Cleopatra and then of the pharaohs Ramesses and Thutmose.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-egyptian">Ancient Egyptian · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li><li><blockquote>French soldiers found the Rosetta Stone, with its decree in hieroglyphs, demotic and Greek, in 1799, and in 1822 <b>Champollion</b>, who knew Coptic well, showed that hieroglyphs combined signs for sounds and for meanings: Egyptian's last stage, kept alive in the liturgy, helped to read its oldest script.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-written-word">The Written Word</a></li></ul></section>
